# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Gauge Mark Seven Bends Toward the Fifth

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 112 BPM and opens with one dry kick every beat and no chord. Minh An begins on a high natural harmonic, then slowly pulls the đàn bầu rod until the pitch approaches a fifth-related target. Mara raises tape-echo feedback throughout the bend but withholds harmony. Only when the pitch stops moving does she strike a short minor-seventh chord, sending it into a filtered delay tail.

Lan Vy answers the stable arrival with one damped lithophone fifth. Quốc Huy replaces the next electronic hat with a brushed frame-drum stroke. Helena remains silent until the second major bend, then enters after the sub is muted with one contrabassoon fundamental that lasts less than a bar.

At the midpoint, Minh An repeats the same starting harmonic but takes twice as long to reach the target. The electronic grid remains unchanged, making the chord arrival feel dramatically late without any tempo shift. The final bend stops just short of full resolution, so the expected chord never appears.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close image of an enamel tide ruler marked 7 beside the flexible rod of a đàn bầu, a hand-drawn line curving from one harmonic node toward an open fifth, reel-to-reel tape heads and pale stone bar in shallow focus. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “mark seven,” “still bending,” “wait for the fifth,” and “do not strike yet.”

## Track 2: Echo Returns After the Waterline Drops

**Story & Context:** The second track runs at 116 BPM with a slightly firmer dub-techno pulse. Mara programs a four-bar chord pattern but sends only alternate stabs into tape echo. Minh An plays short two-note harmonic bends between the dry chords, causing each delayed return to land against a subtly different pitch center.

Lan Vy uses two prepared lithophone notes a minor second apart, striking the higher one only when the echo feedback exceeds three audible repeats. Quốc Huy adds one muted bass frame-drum stroke at the end of every eight-bar cycle. Helena enters in the final half with breath-heavy contrabassoon fifths while the electronic sub disappears completely.

The arrangement progressively removes direct signals until, for sixteen bars, almost everything audible is delayed material, lithophone, and contrabassoon. The direct kick returns first, followed by one clean đàn bầu harmonic with no echo at all.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old paper tidal chart showing a descending waterline beneath three hand-drawn echo repeats, dark reel-to-reel tape loop crossing the page, lithophone bars reflected in a small observation window. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “waterline down,” “third return,” “direct signal gone,” and “bring the dry note back.”

## Track 3: The Rod Stops Moving Before Low Tide

**Story & Context:** The closer begins at 108 BPM with the least electronic low end on the EP. Minh An sustains a low harmonic and moves the tension rod in extremely small increments while Mara leaves long empty spaces between kicks. Each time the rod stops, Lan Vy places one dry stone note and Quốc Huy answers with a fingertip frame-drum tap.

Helena carries the deepest register for nearly two minutes with sparse contrabassoon fundamentals while the sub remains absent. Mara gradually increases the delay time on one muted chord so its repeats drift farther apart, creating the impression that the room itself is slowing down.

In the final section, the tape echo is physically muted, the drum machine loses its hats, and the contrabassoon leaves. Minh An performs one final upward bend, then stops moving the rod before reaching the intended harmonic target. The recording ends on the unstable pitch and the faint mechanical click of the tide-gauge recorder advancing its paper chart.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** A still đàn bầu pitch rod in close focus beside a tide chart approaching its lowest point, one mechanical chart-recorder pen poised above paper, dark water beyond a narrow concrete window. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “rod stopped,” “not low tide yet,” “leave the target open,” and “paper keeps moving.”

